Should Pseudo-Labels Share Mini-Batches with Labeled Examples?Semi-Supervised Continual Active Learning with Limited Training Cost Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2610.35345v1 [cs.LG] 26 Oct 2026 Should Pseudo-Labels Share Mini-Batches with Labeled Examples? Semi-Supervised Continual Active Learning with Limited Training Cost Shiqi Zhang Tuomas Virtanen Abstract Active learning (AL) reduces annotation by querying informative examples. Continual active learning (CAL) also cuts the training cost, the total number of gradient steps, by training one model across AL rounds with a fixed number of mini-batch steps per round. Pseudo-labels add supervision without annotation, but they increase training cost. In CAL, where every round trains on a fixed number of mini-batches, whether and how pseudo-labeled examples should share these mini-batches with labeled examples is therefore open. We provide the first formalization of this problem as two decisions after each query, namely (i) which unlabeled examples should enter training with a pseudo-label and (ii) what share of each mini-batch they should take. Our method, semi-supervised continual active learning (SSCAL), works with any acquisition function. It admits pseudo-labels based on distances from unlabeled examples to labeled neighbors, caps their share, and reweights the loss on labeled examples to keep their expected gradient contribution unchanged. On frozen embeddings, its pseudo-labeler updates incrementally without extra model forward passes. Across two audio datasets, two frozen encoders, six acquisition functions and ten seeds, SSCAL ranks first on average among four protocols with equal training steps, answering the question affirmatively. Index Terms: Audio classification, active learning, continual learning, pseudo-labeling, semi-supervised learning † † address: Faculty of Information Technology and Communication Sciences Tampere University, Tampere, Finland 1 Introduction Active learning (AL) selects informative examples to label and thereby reduces the annotation that model training needs. In audio, for instance, AL has studied clustering with label propagation [ 1 ] , confidence sampling [ 2 ] , and disagreement with diversity [ 3 ] . These advances target annotation efficiency. As models grow larger and deeper, the training cost, the total number of gradient steps spent over all AL rounds, becomes a concern of its own. In AL this cost also delays annotation. When the queries depend on the model, the annotator needs to wait for training in every round before labeling the next queries. Conventional AL protocols retrain for a fixed number of epochs in each AL round, so their training steps are proportional to the labeled set, which grows every round, and the cumulative cost over T T rounds grows quadratically in T T . To avoid this quadratic cost, continual active learning (CAL) [ 4 ] continues training the same model across rounds instead of reinitializing it (model continuation) and keeps training on earlier labels alongside the new ones (replay). The simplest replay in CAL combines new labeled examples with uniformly sampled earlier ones in every mini-batch [ 4 ] . Uniform replay, which samples every labeled example with equal probability, matches or outperforms costlier sampling and distillation methods in fixed-step continual learning [ 5 ] . Semi-supervised learning has long combined the supervised and pseudo-label losses within each mini-batch, with a scheduled weight on the pseudo-label loss [ 6 ] or a fixed ratio of unlabeled to labeled examples [ 7 ] . Its pseudo-labels come from the predictions of the model being trained [ 6 , 7 ] or from labeled neighbors in a feature space, by label propagation over a graph of the examples [ 8 , 9 , 10 ] or by nearest-neighbor assignment from labeled support examples [ 11 ] . Semi-supervised AL borrows this practice to also train on unlabeled examples, through consistency regularization [ 12 ] , pseudo-labels [ 13 , 14 ] or self-training [ 15 ] . In both lines of work the labeled set is fixed during training and the training cost is usually unconstrained, so pseudo-labeled examples add computation on top of the labeled ones and crowd out none of them. Inspired by both lines of work, we study whether pseudo-labels can also help CAL when every round is fixed at K K gradient steps on stochastic mini-batches of m m examples, which keeps the cumulative cost linear in T T . We call this setting fixed-step CAL. Under this constraint, pseudo-labels replace labeled supervision instead of adding to it. A round samples exactly K ​ m Km examples into its mini-batches, so each pseudo-labeled example among them replaces a labeled one. Labeled and pseudo-labeled examples thus share every fixed-size mini-batch. The displacement worsens once the labeled set grows past K ​ m Km , after which a round samples fewer examples than those are labeled, and pseudo-labels, which can be incorrect, crowd out supervision that is already insufficient. Whether pseudo-labels are worth admitting, and what share of each mini-batch they may take, thus depends on how accurate and numerous the admitted pseudo-labels are and on how much labeled supervision they displace. Without a training constraint this trade-off does not arise, so the additive gains of semi-supervised learning do not settle it. We therefore examine this mini-batch sharing in AL at an unchanged annotation budget, where labels are scarce, arrive incrementally, and change every round which pseudo-labels the labeled examples support. We formalize this problem as two decisions taken after each query and define the corresponding protocol. First, pseudo-label admission decides which unlabeled examples enter training with a pseudo-label. Second, mini-batch allocation decides how the mini-batches are shared by setting the sampling distribution over labeled and pseudo-labeled examples. We propose semi-supervised continual active learning (SSCAL), a method that works with any acquisition function. It admits a pseudo-label when an unlabeled example lies closer to two labeled positives of a class than to the nearest labeled negative of that class, caps the sampling share of pseudo-labeled examples, and reweights the loss on labeled examples to keep their expected gradient contribution equal to that of uniform replay. Its pseudo-labeler runs incrementally on frozen embeddings without extra model forward passes. Among four protocols with equal training steps, SSCAL attains the highest area under the learning curve in 202 of 240 paired runs and a mean rank of 1.18. Our contributions are (i) to our knowledge, the first study to bring pseudo-labels into fixed-step CAL, formalized as pseudo-label admission and mini-batch allocation with a corresponding protocol; (ii) SSCAL, an acquisition-function-agnostic method for both decisions; and (iii) experimental validation that answers the mini-batch sharing question affirmatively. 2 Problem and protocol Pool-based AL starts with a pool 𝒟 = { x 1 , … , x N } \mathcal{D}=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}\} of N N examples x i x_{i} , a fixed label space of C C classes, and an annotator who returns the label y i y_{i} of any queried example. All examples are available for training and for computing queries, whereas labels are available only for queried examples. Pseudo-labels may use the acquired labels, the examples themselves and the current model. AL round t t starts from the labeled set L t − 1 L_{t-1} , where L 0 L_{0} is a random initial labeled set of n 0 n_{0} examples, acquires labels for a query set Q t Q_{t} of b b unlabeled examples chosen by the acquisition function, and sets L t = L t − 1 ​ ∪ ˙ ​ Q t L_{t}=L_{t-1}\dot{\cup}Q_{t} and U t = 𝒟 ∖ L t U_{t}=\mathcal{D}\setmin